{"baseRefName":"stack/4-create-subgraph","baseRefOid":"f064001906f45621462b1023494a8a50b15c0b85","body":"Genie –\n\n> Closes #399 · Implements #328 step 7\n> \n> 5 of 6.\n> \n> ### What it establishes\n> - **The node declares.** `declaredPorts()` returns the node's whole interface as an ordered list, computed from its settings, with no side effects.\n> - **The registry reconciles.** `PortRegistry.reconcile` keeps matching ports, replaces retyped ones in place under the same id and position, adds missing ones and removes the rest. It returns a diff, and no longer touches the graph.\n> - **The node applies the diff.** `reconcilePorts()` passes it to the graph: wires, publishing events, and the parameter group rebuilt in port order.\n> \n> Equal settings give equal ports, in equal order, under equal ids for surviving keys, however the node got there.\n> \n> Every node is migrated. Removed: `removePort`, `replacePort`, `reorderPorts`, the base-class port helpers, 26 static name sets and about 35 hand-kept order lists. `addDynamicPort` is deprecated.\n> \n> The only exception: Math Expression, JavaScript and Live Image keep the interface of the last source that compiled, so a half-typed edit keeps its wires.\n> \n> ### Tests\n> - `DeclaredPortsInvariantTests` runs over 50 node types through seeded sequences of settings changes, undos and redos. After each step it compares ports with a node opened from the current settings.\n> - A test opens a document saved with drifted port order, and checks it reads in declared order with every wire kept.\n> \n> ### Not in this PR\n> - Clone sets (#6).","files":[{"path":"AGENTS.md","additions":10,"deletions":4,"changeType":"MODIFIED"},{"path":"Fabric/Graph/Graph+SerializationDiagnostics.swift","additions":1,"deletions":1,"changeType":"MODIFIED"},{"path":"Fabric/Graph/Graph.swift","additions":5,"deletions":2,"changeType":"MODIFIED"},{"path":"Fabric/Graph/Node/Node+DeviceOutputPorts.swift","additions":12,"deletions":39,"changeType":"MODI